(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-asthma-inhalers-status-quiz"]={"title":"Asthma Steps, Inhaler Devices & Status Asthmaticus Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which controller appears only among the other controller options, never as a preferred controller, in stepwise asthma therapy?","choices":["Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist","A leukotriene receptor antagonist","Medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist","As-needed low-dose inhaled corticosteroid-formoterol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A leukotriene receptor antagonist (such as montelukast) is listed only among the other controller options, at steps 2 to 4. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does ketamine produce bronchodilation in status asthmaticus?","choices":["It blocks muscarinic receptors","It increases catecholamine release","It inhibits phosphodiesterase","It stabilizes mast cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ketamine, an N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor antagonist and dissociative anesthetic, increases catecholamine release, which causes bronchodilation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"When a valve holding chamber is used, when should the patient begin breathing in?","choices":["Within 5 seconds after pressing down","At the same moment as pressing down","Before pressing down on the inhaler","After waiting at least half a minute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a holding chamber the patient first presses down on the inhaler and then begins breathing in within 5 seconds. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A patient remains uncontrolled on high-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist. What does stepwise asthma therapy list next?","choices":["Switching to theophylline tablets","Stopping the long-acting agent","Referral for phenotypic assessment","Switching to a mast cell stabilizer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At the top step the patient is referred for phenotypic assessment, with possible add-on therapy such as tiotropium or a biologic (anti-immunoglobulin E, anti-interleukin-5, anti-interleukin-4 receptor). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A patient's asthma is not controlled on a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid. Which preferred controller is the next step up?","choices":["Medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist","As-needed low-dose inhaled corticosteroid-formoterol","Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist","Long-acting beta-2 agonist on its own, no steroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. From daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (step 2) the next preferred controller is the low-dose combination with a long-acting beta-2 agonist (step 3). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Without a holding chamber, how should the patient breathe in while pressing down on the inhaler?","choices":["Quickly and forcefully","Slowly and deeply","In short, shallow sips","Through the nose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The patient starts breathing in slowly as the inhaler is pressed down and breathes in slowly and deeply, then holds the breath. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which device uses a compressor to force liquid medication into a fine mist?","choices":["Metered dose inhaler","Dry powder inhaler","Valve holding chamber","Nebulizer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a nebulizer a compressor forces liquid medication into a fine mist, allowing passive inhalation through the mouthpiece or mask. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Why can nebulized treatment lead to more side effects than other inhaled delivery?","choices":["It requires the most coordination","It retains the larger particles","It needs strong inspiratory force","A bigger dose is given overall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A nebulizer administers a bigger dose overall, which may lead to more side effects, although its passive inhalation suits children and older adults who cannot use an inhaler. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"In the status asthmaticus algorithm, when is ketamine listed?","choices":["Before any albuterol is given","In place of steroids","For mild intermittent asthma","If the patient is intubated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ketamine is listed in status asthmaticus if the patient is intubated; it increases catecholamine release, causing bronchodilation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A patient with asthma has needed albuterol many times a day for several weeks. What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["Good control; keep the same plan","Poor control; adjust long-term therapy","A need for more albuterol per puff","A reason to stop the controller"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Albuterol provides only short-term relief and should be used sparingly; excessive use indicates poor control and the need to adjust long-term therapy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What does a spacer or valve holding chamber do to the particles the patient inhales?","choices":["Raises the share of large, heavy particles","Turns the liquid medication into a powder","Raises the share of small, respirable particles","Raises the total dose that is delivered"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spacers and valve holding chambers are intended to retain the large particles emitted from the inhaler, leaving a higher proportion of smaller, respirable particles. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is listed for ketamine in status asthmaticus?","choices":["Emergence phenomenon","Hypotension and bradycardia","Oral candidiasis","Red-green color loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ketamine's listed adverse reactions are emergence phenomenon, hypertension, tachycardia and vomiting. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"In a dry powder inhaler, what draws the medication out of the device?","choices":["The patient's inspiratory force","A propellant in the canister","An electric compressor","A one-way valve chamber"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a dry powder inhaler the medication is a powder, and the patient provides the inspiratory force that draws it out of the device. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How is albuterol given in the status asthmaticus algorithm?","choices":["Continuously, by nebulizer","By dry powder inhaler","As a twice-daily inhaler","As a single oral tablet"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The status asthmaticus algorithm gives continuous albuterol, nebulized, together with steroids, magnesium and, if the patient is intubated, ketamine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"}]};
